ASoC: sun4i-codec: h713: add the I2S input path for the MSP DSP

H713's DAC can take its samples from somewhere other than the APB
FIFO. A second register window sits right behind the codec, at
0x02031000 and 0x7c long, and holds an I2S receiver wired to the DAC.
That receiver is how the audio the SoC's MSP DSP decodes from HDMI
reaches the speaker: the DSP is an island of its own with no path into
DRAM, so its output is an I2S stream and nothing else, and there is no
PCM anywhere for ALSA to open.

Everything the vendor kernel does with that window was read out of the
stock 5.4 vmlinux, which carries full symbols and DWARF --
sunxi_codec_i2s_init() @0xc0845230 with its 21 writes,
sunxi_codec_set_dacsrc_mode() @0xc0844fbc for the switch-over, and the
i2s_reg_labels table @0xc149ce98 plus sunxi_i2s_regmap_config
@0xc0ee8a54, which name every field and put the end of the map at 0x7c
(S25 §1, §2, §3, §4.2). The block turns out to be an ordinary
sunxi-daudio register map, and the codec is its clock master while the
DSP follows.

The whole path was then run by hand on the board on 08.09.2026: with
the window programmed this way, the DAC switched over and the analog
side held up by a silent APB stream, a 1 kHz tone from an HDMI source
came out of the speaker at 80.2 dB (S16 20:30). That measurement is
what this patch turns into driver code, and it decides two design
points.

First, the analog side. The vendor's mixer path writes DAC_DPC's
DAC_EN, both halves of DAC_REG, the headphone amplifier the speaker
hangs off and the PA GPIO by hand. All four already have DAPM widgets
here, and DAPM only powers them for a running PCM. So instead of
writing them a second time -- and having to decide by hand what to do
when a PCM and the I2S input want them at the same time -- the path
gets a signal generator in front of the DACs, which "DAC Source"
forces on. DAPM then powers up exactly what it powers up for playback
and, because it counts every reason a widget is needed, it keeps the
analog side alive for whichever of the two still wants it when the
other goes away. The generator is disconnected in the card's late
probe, before the first sync, or a source with no off switch would
power the DAC from boot.

Second, the rate. The receiver is the clock master with fixed
dividers, so nothing about it follows the incoming stream: the DAC's
own rate has to be set to the rate of the HDMI source. Getting it
wrong is silent rather than merely detuned -- a 32 kHz source stayed
inaudible with the codec left at 48 kHz and came back at 79.6 dB once
the codec was moved to 32 kHz as well (S16 20:45). "I2S Rate"
therefore offers the three rates that were measured end to end, and
programs them exactly as hw_params() and prepare() do for an APB
stream: the 44.1 kHz family on a 45.1584 MHz module clock and the rest
on 49.152 MHz, both read back from the clock tree while the tone was
audible.

The two paths share the module clock and the DAC's rate field and
cannot be given different rates at once. Rather than let one silently
retune the other, hw_params() refuses an APB stream whose rate differs
from the armed I2S path, and the two controls refuse to change a rate
out from under a stream that is already open. Nothing else about the
APB path changes: the I2S path holds the module clock through the
clock framework's own reference count, so a stream that starts or
stops while HDMI audio is playing neither gates the clock nor powers
the amplifier down.

Old device trees describe only the DAC window. That is not an error
here, it just means there is no I2S input: the driver warns, leaves
both controls out, and the APB path works as before.

Register values carry their source in the comments. The sequence has
no delays anywhere, and going back to the APB FIFO clears the selector
bit alone, both as in the vendor's code.

Hauptsitzung 08.09. 23:45: I2S-Regmap ohne Geraetebindung (regmap_init_mmio(NULL)) --
dev_get_regmap(dev, NULL) lieferte sonst die I2S-Karte an die ASoC-Komponente
(-EIO auf 0x310, Analogseite blieb aus).
